DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ppww utility is a command-line based editor for the system _u_s_e_r and
     _g_r_o_u_p files, allowing the superuser an easy to use and standardized way
     of adding, modifying and removing users and groups.  Note that ppww only
     operates on the local user and group files.  NIS users and groups must be
     maintained on the NIS server.  The ppww utility handles updating the
     passwd(5), master.passwd(5), group(5) and the secure and insecure pass‐
     word database files, and must be run as root.

     The first one or two keywords provided to ppww on the command line provide
     the context for the remainder of the arguments.  The keywords uusseerr and
     ggrroouupp may be combined with aadddd, ddeell, mmoodd, sshhooww, or nneexxtt in any order.
     (For example, sshhoowwuusseerr, uusseerrsshhooww, sshhooww uusseerr, and uusseerr sshhooww all mean the
     same thing.)  This flexibility is useful for interactive scripts calling
     ppww for user and group database manipulation.  Following these keywords,
     the user or group name or numeric id may be optionally specified as an
     alternative to using the --nn _n_a_m_e, --uu _u_i_d, --gg _g_i_d options.

     The following flags are common to most or all modes of operation:

     --RR _r_o_o_t_d_i_r    Specifies an alternate root directory within which ppww will
                   operate.  Any paths specified will be relative to _r_o_o_t_d_i_r.

     --VV _e_t_c_d_i_r     Set an alternate location for the password, group, and con‐
                   figuration files.  Can be used to maintain a user/group
                   database in an alternate location.  If this switch is spec‐
                   ified, the system _/_e_t_c_/_p_w_._c_o_n_f will not be sourced for de‐
                   fault configuration data, but the file _p_w_._c_o_n_f in the spec‐
                   ified directory will be used instead (or none, if it does
                   not exist).  The --CC flag may be used to override this be‐
                   haviour.  As an exception to the general rule where options
                   must follow the operation type, the --VV flag must be used on
                   the command line before the operation keyword.

     --CC _c_o_n_f_i_g     By default, ppww reads the file _/_e_t_c_/_p_w_._c_o_n_f to obtain policy
                   information on how new user accounts and groups are to be
                   created.  The --CC option specifies a different configuration
                   file.  While most of the contents of the configuration file
                   may be overridden via command-line options, it may be more
                   convenient to keep standard information in a configuration
                   file.

     --qq            Use of this option causes ppww to suppress error messages,
                   which may be useful in interactive environments where it is
                   preferable to interpret status codes returned by ppww rather
                   than messing up a carefully formatted display.

     --NN            This option is available in aadddd and mmooddiiffyy operations, and
                   tells ppww to output the result of the operation without up‐
                   dating the user or group databases.  You may use the --PP op‐
                   tion to switch between standard passwd and readable for‐
                   mats.

     --YY            Using this option with any of the update modes causes ppww to
                   run make(1) after changing to the directory _/_v_a_r_/_y_p.  This
                   is intended to allow automatic updating of NIS database
                   files.  If separate passwd and group files are being used
                   by NIS, then use the --yy _n_i_s_p_a_s_s_w_d option to specify the lo‐
                   cation of the NIS passwd database so that ppww will concur‐
                   rently update it with the system password databases.

UUSSEERR OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options apply to the uusseerraadddd and uusseerrmmoodd commands:

     [--nn] _n_a_m_e     Required unless --uu _u_i_d is given.  Specify the user/account
                   name.  In the case of uusseerrmmoodd can be a _u_i_d.

     --uu _u_i_d        Required if _n_a_m_e is not given.  Specify the user/account
                   numeric id.  In the case of uusseerrmmoodd if paired with _n_a_m_e,
                   changes the numeric id of the named user/account.

                   Usually, only one of these options is required, as the ac‐
                   count name will imply the uid, or vice versa.  However,
                   there are times when both are needed.  For example, when
                   changing the uid of an existing user with uusseerrmmoodd, or over‐
                   riding the default uid when creating a new account with
                   uusseerraadddd.  To automatically allocate the uid to a new user
                   with uusseerraadddd, then do _n_o_t use the --uu option.  Either the
                   account or userid can also be provided immediately after
                   the uusseerraadddd, uusseerrddeell, uusseerrmmoodd, or uusseerrsshhooww keywords on the
                   command line without using the --nn or --uu options.

     --cc _c_o_m_m_e_n_t    This field sets the contents of the passwd GECOS field,
                   which normally contains up to four comma-separated fields
                   containing the user's full name, office or location, and
                   work and home phone numbers.  These sub-fields are used by
                   convention only, however, and are optional.  If this field
                   is to contain spaces, the comment must be enclosed in dou‐
                   ble quotes ‘"’.  Avoid using commas in this field as these
                   are used as sub-field separators, and the colon ‘:’ charac‐
                   ter also cannot be used as this is the field separator for
                   the passwd file itself.

     --dd _h_o_m_e_d_i_r    This option sets the account's home directory.  Normally,
                   this is only used if the home directory is to be different
                   from the default determined from _/_e_t_c_/_p_w_._c_o_n_f - normally
                   _/_h_o_m_e with the account name as a subdirectory.

     --ee _a_c_c_e_x_p_d_a_t_e
                   Set the account's expiration date.  Format of the date is
                   either a UNIX time in decimal, or a date in ‘dd-mmm-yy[yy]’
                   format, where dd is the day, mmm is the month, either in
                   numeric or alphabetic format ('Jan', 'Feb', etc) and year
                   is either a two or four digit year.  This option also ac‐
                   cepts a relative date in the form ‘+n[mhdwoy]’ where ‘n’ is
                   a decimal, octal (leading 0) or hexadecimal (leading 0x)
                   digit followed by the number of Minutes, Hours, Days,
                   Weeks, Months or Years from the current date at which the
                   expiration date is to be set.

     --pp _p_a_s_s_e_x_p_d_a_t_e
                   Set the account's password expiration date.  This field is
                   similar to the account expiration date option, except that
                   it applies to forced password changes.  This is set in the
                   same manner as the --ee option.

     --gg _g_r_o_u_p      Set the account's primary group to the given group.  _g_r_o_u_p
                   may be defined by either its name or group number.

     --GG _g_r_o_u_p_l_i_s_t  Set secondary group memberships for an account.  _g_r_o_u_p_l_i_s_t
                   is a comma, space, or tab-separated list of group names or
                   group numbers.  The user is added to the groups specified
                   in _g_r_o_u_p_l_i_s_t, and removed from all groups not specified.
                   The current login session is not affected by group member‐
                   ship changes, which only take effect when the user recon‐
                   nects.  Note: do not add a user to their primary group with
                   _g_r_o_u_p_l_i_s_t.

     --LL _c_l_a_s_s      This option sets the login class for the user being cre‐
                   ated.  See login.conf(5) and passwd(5) for more information
                   on user login classes.

